## 5. "Locality" logic

`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerLocality.java` — full content:

```java
public final class OwnerLocality {
    private static final Map<String, String> CITY_REGION = Map.of(
            "Sydney", "NSW", "Melbourne", "VIC", "Brisbane", "QLD");
    private OwnerLocality() {}
    public static String forPostcode(String postcode, String city) {
        String byPostcode = byPostcode(postcode);
        if (byPostcode != null) { return byPostcode; }
        return CITY_REGION.getOrDefault(city, "UNKNOWN");
    }
    public static String region(Owner owner) {
        String code = owner.getCustomerCode();
        int dash = code == null ? -1 : code.indexOf('-');
        return dash < 0 ? "UNKNOWN" : code.substring(0, dash);
    }
    private static String byPostcode(String postcode) {
        if (postcode == null) { return null; }
        int value = Integer.parseInt(postcode);
        if (value >= 2000 && value <= 2099) { return "NSW"; }
        if (value >= 3000 && value <= 3099) { return "VIC"; }
        if (value >= 4000 && value <= 4099) { return "QLD"; }
        return null;
    }
}
```

The DTO/response `locality` field is populated in `OwnerMapper.java` line 25 via `OwnerLocality.region(owner)` — which reads the region back off the `customerCode` prefix (`<REGION>-<HASH>`), NOT off the city directly. Note the OpenAPI `locality` description (line 2178) still says it is "derived from their city (Sydney->NSW ...)", which is now only indirectly true. `forPostcode(...)` is a separate helper used when building the customerCode identity.

